TI  - A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial of vitamin A in Mozambican children hospitalized with non-measles acute lower respiratory tract infections
PY  - 1999
AB  - This randomized double-blind placebo-controlled clinical trial tested the potential of routine administration of vitamin A at admission to speed up recovery during hospitalization for acute lower respiratory tract infections (ALRIs) and to decrease morbidity at 6 weeks after discharge. The sample population included children aged 6-72 months with ALRIs admitted to the pediatric wards of the Central Hospital of Maputo Mozambique from 1995-97. These children were assigned to a supplementation group (n = 71 receiving 200000 IU of vitamin A) or a control group (n = 93 receiving a placebo). Analysis revealed that both groups had a very high and similar prevalence of vitamin A deficiency. The median number of inpatient days for the supplementation group was 3; 4 days for the placebo group. In addition the rate of clinical discharge on day 5 was 88.4% (n = 61/69) in the experimental intervention group and 73.9% (n = 65/88) in the placebo group (P = 0.023). Findings indicate a statistically significant reduction in duration of days of admission among vitamin A-supplemented children with ALRIs.
