AU  - Bloem, Martin W.; Wedel, Michel; Egger, Robert J.; Speek, Andries J.; Schrijver, J.; Saowakontha, Sastri; Schreurs, Wil H.P.
TI  - MILD VITAMIN A DEFICIENCY AND RISK OF RESPIRATORY TRACT DISEASES AND DIARRHEA IN PRESCHOOL AND SCHOOL CHILDREN IN NORTHEASTERN THAILAND
PY  - 1990
AB  - The author challenges the results and conclusions of a recent study by Bloem et al. confirming findings of Sommer et al. Results of the cross-sectional study showing an association between history of diarrhea or respiratory disease and low retinol values should be further analyzed and conclusions reevaluated. More specifically Bloem et al.s findings that children with respiratory disease demonstrated mean retinol levels significantly lower than children with diarrhea and free of disease may be confounded by the effect of fever on retinol levels. To more accurately compare retinol levels between disease and disease-free subjects Bloem et al. should not include fever in the definition of respiratory disease. The author also questions the studys dose-dependent relation between Vitamin A and the incidence of respiratory disease. Result confounding by age may be possible given the inverse relationship of respiratory disease and Vitamin A deficiency to age. Bloem et al.s Table 3 should control for age. Given these concerns over inconsistent results the author questions whether there is any impact of Vitamin A supplementation on diarrhea incidence. Finally the author calls for identification of the cause of large differences between the cross-sectional and follow-up studies rates of diarrhea and respiratory disease. He also finds Bloem et al.s total sample size to not add up as it should and disagrees that the study confirms Sommer et al.s findings.
